.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO 3 "2026-07-11" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do \- do an HTTP request and buffer the full response
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do(chttpcli cli,
                             const chttp_request_t *req,
                             chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function takes a pool slot. It blocks while every slot is in
use. It then runs the HTTP request synchronously and buffers the whole
response body. It puts a chttpcli_response on the heap into
*resp_out.

The function sets *resp_out to NULL at once, before it starts any
other work. *resp_out stays NULL on every return that is not a
success. You can therefore always call chttpclient_resp_free(resp)
after this call, whatever ccol_retval_t it gives back. You do not need
to set your own local pointer before the call. After a success the
caller owns *resp_out. The caller must call chttpclient_resp_free on
it at the end.

The client follows a redirect automatically. The redirect codes are
301, 302, 303, 307 and 308, and the client follows up to 50 hops. For
301, 302 and 303 it rewrites the method to a GET with no body. It
leaves HEAD as HEAD. For 307 and 308 it keeps the original method and
the original body. The Location header can hold several forms. It can
be an absolute URL. It can be a protocol-relative reference
("//host/path"). It can be an absolute-path reference ("/foo"). It can
also be a general relative reference, such as "foo", "../foo", "./foo"
or "?query". The client resolves all of them as RFC 3986 asks. A
Location value with a scheme of its own is always absolute, as RFC
3986 SS5.2.2 says. Examples are "mailto:x@y", "ftp://host/path" and
any scheme other than http, https and http+unix. Such a value resolves
to itself without a change. This client connects only over http, https
and http+unix. The next hop therefore reports ccol_http_invalid_url.
An unsupported scheme in the original request URL gets that same code.
The client does not merge the reference onto the path of the current
origin as if the reference were relative. A response with a redirect
code and no Location header, or an empty one, is not a redirect: the
client delivers it as the final response, as the Go net/http client
does.

A redirect from https to http is followed by default. That matches curl,
whose CURLOPT_REDIR_PROTOCOLS permits both schemes, and the Go net/http
client.

Set chttp_request_t.prevent_tls_downgrade_on_redirect to refuse such a redirect
instead. The client then reports ccol_http_invalid_url and opens no
plaintext connection. Weigh that against the default: the peer that sent
the Location chooses the downgrade, and once a chain leaves TLS that hop
and every later hop travel in clear.

A Location that merely inherits the scheme, such as "//host/path" or
"/path", resolves back to https and follows whatever that field holds.
So does an upgrade from http to https, which can only add protection.

The rule below, for AF_UNIX, is not optional and no field relaxes it.

A hop can name a Unix domain
socket ("http+unix://...") only in one case: the hop that carried the
Location was bound to that same socket path. The client refuses every
other resolved http+unix:// target with ccol_http_invalid_url, and it
tries no connection. The rule holds for every hop, against the hop
that the Location came from. A chain that leaves a socket therefore
cannot come back to it. A chain that never used a socket cannot reach
one. The length of the chain makes no difference. The match of the
scheme ignores case. The client compares the socket path after it
decodes the percent escapes. A different spelling or a second encoding
therefore cannot get past the rule. A relative Location on a Unix hop
still resolves back onto the socket of that hop, and the client
follows it in the normal way. Without this rule, any http or https
server can redirect a caller onto any local socket. That server also
controls the target of the request.

The client can reach the cap of 50 hops. If the response of the last
hop is itself a redirect, the client does not follow it and does not
deliver it. The client gives ccol_http_too_many_redirects instead.
The normalization of the dot segments ".." and "." only rewrites the
path part. The client never treats "/", ".." or "." inside a query
string as path navigation. The request URL can carry credentials (see
below). The client then makes an Authorization header from them. It
sends that header again on every hop that stays on the same origin.
An origin is a scheme, a host and a port. The host compares without
regard to case, and the scheme and the port compare exactly. The
client drops the header for good the first time a hop changes the
origin.

The headers that you set with chttp_request_set_header follow a
redirect with two exceptions, which are the rules of curl. The client
judges every hop against the ORIGINAL request, and never against the
hop before it. A hop whose scheme, host or port differs from the
original request carries none of your Authorization, Cookie, Cookie2
and WWW-Authenticate headers. A hop whose host differs also carries
none of your Host header, and the client writes its own Host line for
the new target instead. Hosts and header names match without regard to
case. A
hop that comes back to the original origin therefore carries those
headers again, and a second hop on the same foreign origin still
carries none of them. Every other header of yours follows every hop.

A 301, 302 or 303 hop rewrites the method to a GET with no body. Such
a hop also removes every Content-Length, Content-Type and Expect
header that the caller set. It removes them from that hop and from
every hop after it. A later 307 or 308 hop can bring a real body back,
and then the client sends those headers again. Those three headers
only describe a body. A value that described the body of the original
request is never correct on a request with no body at all. The client
sends every other header of the caller again on every hop, without a
change.

The request URL accepts http://, https:// and http+unix://. The
support for a Unix domain socket is the same in all three tiers. The
form with the encoded socket path is
"http+unix://%2Fpath%2Fto%2Fsock/path". This matches the convention of
requests-unixsocket in Python. For the two network forms the client
accepts a plain hostname and an IPv4 literal. It also accepts an
IPv6 literal inside brackets ("https://[::1]:8443/path"). A URL can
also carry credentials
("http://user:pass@host/path"). The client turns them into an
"Authorization: Basic ..." header for you. It does not do this if the
request already sets its own Authorization header. This does not apply
to http+unix://, which has no convention for userinfo. The client also
finds a "#fragment" at the end of a URL and drops it. It never sends a
fragment to a server.

An empty port, as in "http://host:/", names the default port of the
scheme (RFC 3986 section 3.2.3), as it does in curl. Port 0 is
refused. A fully qualified hostname with one trailing dot, as in
"https://www.example.com./", is resolved and sent in the Host header
exactly as written. TLS uses the name without that dot for SNI and for
the check of the certificate, as curl and Go do. The keep-alive cache
matches hosts without regard to case. A name with a trailing dot and
the same name without it are two origins, because the name without
the dot can resolve through a search domain to another host.

A server can send one response header name on several lines. See
chttpclient_resp_header for the combined value, and
chttpclient_resp_header_at for each line on its own. A redirect with
more than one Location line goes to the first one, as curl and the Go
net/http client do.

The client builds the request-target of the request line from the path
and the query of the URL, and from those of every Location that it
follows. It percent-encodes as "%XX" every byte that RFC 3986 does not
allow there literally: the space, the control bytes, DEL, every byte
of 0x80 and above, and the characters '"', '<', '>', '\\', '^', '`',
\&'{', '|', '}', '[' and ']'. An existing "%XX" escape is kept as it is,
and is never encoded a second time. A '%' that two hex digits do not
follow makes the URL invalid, in your URL and in a Location alike. Go's
net/url rejects the same input. A Location of "/my file" therefore
becomes the request-target "/my%20file".

A host name can resolve to several addresses. The client connects
with Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305), as the default dialer of Go does. It
takes the addresses in the order that the resolver gives, which is the
order of RFC 6724, and alternates the address families, starting with
the family of the first address. It starts the first address at once,
and the next one when the attempts in flight have given no result for
250 ms, or at once when an attempt fails. The first attempt that
connects wins, and the client closes every other one. It reports
ccol_http_connection_failed only after every address failed. The
connect timeout is one budget for the whole race; when it runs out,
the connect ends with ccol_timed_out. An address that drops its
packets therefore costs about 250 ms and not the connect timeout, and
the name "localhost", which a standard /etc/hosts lists as ::1 before
127.0.0.1, reaches a server that listens on 127.0.0.1 alone.
chttpclient_do starts the next address exactly at 250 ms;
chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_pooled start it between
250 ms and 350 ms after the one before it.

A server can answer before it reads the full request. Examples are a
401 from a check of credentials and a 413 from a size limit. The server
can then close the connection or stop the read. Each tier monitors the
connection for such an answer while it sends the request. Each tier
gives that answer, as curl and the net/http client of Go do. The
rules are:
  - The client drops an interim (1xx) response and continues the send.
  - A final response of 300 or above stops the send when its header
    block has arrived. The client does not send the remaining part of
    the request.
  - A final response of 200 to 299 lets the send continue while the
    response arrives. The reason is that a server can stream its answer
    while it reads the request. A complete response stops the send.
  - A write can fail because the peer went away. This stops the send.
    The client then gives the answer that the peer sent before it
    went, if there is one.
The client never reuses a connection whose response started before the
client sent the full request.

On Linux, a reset discards the data that the client did not read. The
client therefore gives an answer only when it reads that answer
before a reset arrives. chttpclient_do waits for readability during each wait of
the send. chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_pooled look for an
answer at these times:
  - When a write would block.
  - After a write, when 32 KiB or more went out since the last look.
A response can arrive while a write of chttpclient_do_async or
chttpclient_do_pooled waits for space in the socket. These functions
see that response in 100 ms or less.

Each socket of the client is close-on-exec. Each file that the TLS
layer of the client reads is also close-on-exec. A process that the
application starts therefore gets no connection of the client.

The server can send an interim informational response with a 1xx
status. The client drops each such response. The client then continues
to read on the same connection for the real response. A caller therefore
never gets a 1xx status as the final answer. "103 Early Hints" of
RFC 8297 is the most frequent example. It can come before an ordinary
response, whatever the value of req->expect_continue.

The client does not accept an infinite sequence of interim responses.
After it drops 64 interim responses in sequence on one hop,
chttpclient_do stops and gives ccol_http_transfer_aborted. This limit is
important, because the request timeout is 0 by default, and 0 means no
timeout.

The client does not drop "101 Switching Protocols". A 101 answers an
Upgrade request, and this client never sends one. The bytes after a 101
belong to a different protocol. A 101 therefore fails the request with
ccol_http_transfer_aborted in every tier, as in curl.

One header line of a response can be up to 64 KiB long, and the whole
header block up to 64 KiB and 100 lines. The status line stays within
8 KiB. A response past any of those limits gives
ccol_http_transfer_aborted. The same limits hold in every tier.

Is req->expect_continue true, and does the request have a body? The
client then sends only the headers of the request first. It then waits
up to one second for the first byte of a response from the server. The
second bounds only that wait: a response that starts inside it is read
to its end, bounded only by the request timeout, however long its rest
takes. The wait ends in one of these ways:

  - The server sends a "100 Continue" interim response. The client
    then sends the body and reads the real final response in the
    normal way. A response that arrives together with the
    "100 Continue" is read before the body goes out, under the
    rules for an answer that arrives while the request is sent
    (see above): a final response of 300 or above, or a complete
    one, ends the request with no byte of the body sent.
  - The server sends some OTHER interim 1xx status while the client
    waits. An example is "103 Early Hints" before "100 Continue".
    The client drops it and waits on for the first byte of the next
    response, inside the same budget of one second.
  - The server answers with a final status instead, for example to
    reject the request and to ask for no body. The client gives that
    response to the caller as it is, and it never sends the body. It
    closes the connection afterwards and never reuses it. This holds
    for a final response that is still arriving when the second ends.
  - The wait of one second ends before any byte of a response. The client
    then sends the body and reads the final response in the normal
    way. It takes the server for one that does not support this
    mechanism.

The cap of 64 dropped interim responses in a row applies to two
phases on their own. The first phase is the wait for "100 Continue".
The second phase is the read of the final response after it. A "100
Continue" is a real outcome, and so is the end of the wait with no
response. Neither is a dropped response, and each one sets the count
back to zero. One hop can therefore drop up to 64 interim responses
during the wait for "100 Continue". It can then drop up to 64 more
during the read of the final response. The total is not 64.

expect_continue has no effect on a request with no body. Every tier
honors it in the same way. chttpclient_do_async and everything on top
of it use the same protocol with three outcomes, which the text above
describes. They make it part of their own dispatch, which does not
block, instead of a wait that blocks.

The client can get a real "100 Continue" interim response and then
write the body on that connection. After that point, the client reports
a later failure on that connection directly. The client never tries the
request again on a different connection. This is also true for a
connection from the keep-alive idle pool. The server showed that it was
alive and that it accepted the body. A retry sends the body a second
time, on a different connection. A request that is not idempotent can
then run two times. "Connection reuse" in chttpclient(7) gives the
general rule that lets the client try a reused connection again one
time. This case is the one exception to that rule.

The response body in the buffer has no size limit by default. Call
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size to cap it. The client then
gives ccol_msg_too_large for a response above that cap, and does not
buffer the whole body.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req       The request to run. It must not be NULL.
resp_out  The output parameter. It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success
       The request finished, and *resp_out is valid.

ccol_invalid_args
       An argument is NULL. Or cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale
       handle of a client that is already destroyed. Or a request
       header holds a CR byte or an LF byte inside it, and the same
       holds for body.content_type on a request with a body. Or a
       request header has the name "Transfer-Encoding". Or, on a
       request that carries a body (POST, PUT or PATCH), a request
       header with the name "Content-Length" holds a bad value. A
       good value is a plain unsigned string of decimal digits. It
       has no leading '+' or '-', and no whitespace inside it. It
       must also match the real length of the body that the client
       sends. See chttp_request_set_header(3).

ccol_not_enough_memory
       An allocation of memory failed.

ccol_timed_out
       The request timeout or the connect timeout fired.

ccol_not_permitted
       The client is in its destroy, and it accepts no new request.

ccol_http_invalid_url
       The URL is malformed. Or it uses an unsupported scheme. The
       client supports only http://, https:// and http+unix://. Or
       its host, port or userinfo part is missing or not valid.

       The host must match the uri-host rule of RFC 3986 section
       3.2.2. That is, the host must be one of these:
         - A name of letters, digits, '-', '.', '_', '~', the
           sub-delims !$&'()*+,;= and %XX escapes.
         - A bracketed IPv6 address or IPvFuture literal.
       These make the URL invalid: a space, a tab, a control byte, a
       byte of 0x80 or above, a backslash, and a bracket that the
       authority does not close. Give a host name outside ASCII in
       its punycode (IDNA) form. The client does not support an IPv6
       zone identifier.

       Credentials in the URL can hold only the characters of the
       userinfo rule, %XX escapes and an unescaped '@' in the
       password. A user name can hold a ':' after the client decodes
       its escapes, as in "http://a%3Ab:pw@host/". Such a user name
       has no valid Basic credential (see chttp_basic_auth(3)). The
       URL is then also invalid.

       These also make the URL invalid: a CR byte or an LF byte in
       the path or in the query, and a '%' in the path or the query
       that two hex digits do not follow. Without these checks, the
       client copies such a byte into the Host header or the request
       line. The URL can then inject more header lines, or smuggle a
       full second request. Each Location that the client follows
       goes through the same rules.

       Or, for http+unix://, the URL gives a socket path that is too
       long for sockaddr_un.sun_path. The client also gives this code
       when a redirect resolves to a http+unix:// target and the hop
       that carried the Location was not bound to it (see
       DESCRIPTION).

ccol_http_host_resolution_failed
       The DNS resolution of the target host failed. The client never
       gives this code for http+unix://, which has no DNS step.

ccol_http_connection_failed
       The client could not make the connection. Every address of the
       host refused it or failed, or a http+unix:// socket path does
       not exist. A socket error on a connection that the client
       already made is ccol_http_transfer_aborted instead.

ccol_http_too_many_redirects
       The chain of redirects went past 50 hops.

ccol_http_tls_handshake_failed
       The TLS handshake failed for a reason other than the
       verification of a certificate.

ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed
       The client could not verify the certificate of the peer or its
       hostname.

ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed
       The client could not read the client certificate, the key or
       the CA bundle path of the configuration. Or the TLS layer could
       not load or parse one of them.

ccol_http_transfer_aborted
       The connection failed during the transfer. Or the server sent
       a malformed HTTP/1.1 response. A response with ambiguous
       framing is malformed. These responses have ambiguous framing:
         - A response with a Content-Length and a Transfer-Encoding
           (RFC 7230 section 3.3.3).
         - A response that applies "chunked" more than one time
           (section 3.3.1). The client reads the codings of all
           Transfer-Encoding lines as one list.
       A Transfer-Encoding without "chunked" and without a
       Content-Length is not ambiguous. The client reads such a body
       until the connection closes. A chunk-size line is also
       malformed when its chunk extension does not obey the grammar
       of RFC 9112 section 7.1.1, for example when it holds a control
       byte.

       Or a streaming chttpcli_write_fn gave back a value that is not
       the number of bytes that it got.

       Over https://, this code also includes a response whose body
       ends at the close of the connection (no Content-Length and no
       chunked framing) when the server closes without a TLS
       close_notify alert. Without that alert, the client cannot tell
       the end of the body from a connection that an attacker or a
       failure stopped too early. The client therefore reports the
       response as truncated. A body with a length or with chunked
       framing does not need the alert.

       A connection that fails while the client sends the request
       gives this code only when the client found no answer from the
       server; see DESCRIPTION. A "101 Switching Protocols" response
       also gives this code.

ccol_msg_too_large
       The response body went past the cap of
       chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size. The client gives this
       code only after you set that cap.

ccol_unexpected_failure
       Any other internal failure that the codes above do not cover.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library leaves the disposition of SIGPIPE to the application.  It
never changes it, in any tier.  No write of the client raises that
signal: every write to a socket, a TLS record and the close_notify of
a TLS teardown included, is a send(2) with MSG_NOSIGNAL.  A server that
closes or resets the connection while the client writes a request
therefore fails that request, and never ends the process.
.fi
